course: geography
lesson: 16
title: The great rivers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A great river collects rain from a huge area, called its basin, through thousands of smaller streams called tributaries, and carries it all to the sea or to a lake.
explain: Which river is the longest is still argued about: the Nile, about 6,650 km, is usually named first, but some measurements make the Amazon a little longer, because nobody agrees exactly where a river begins and ends.
explain: Rivers made history: Egypt grew on the Nile's yearly floods, China on the Yellow River and the Yangtze, and in Europa Universalis 5 the Danube is a frontier between the Habsburgs, Hungary and the Ottomans, while Rhine cities like Cologne grow rich from tolls.
explain: The game makes rivers mostly lines that slow armies down, but real rivers also bring water, rich silt for farms, fish, power and roads for boats.
explain: Think of pouring a bucket of water on a sandpit: little channels join into bigger ones and run downhill to one place. A river basin works the same way, only the size of a country.
fact: The Nile, about 6,650 km, is usually called the world's longest river, but some measurements make the Amazon, about 6,400 km or more, a little longer.
fact: The Amazon flows through rainforest, where heavy rain falls nearly all year, and carries about a fifth of all the river water that flows into the world's oceans, more than the next several largest rivers together.
fact: The White Nile and the Blue Nile meet at Khartoum in Sudan, and the Blue Nile from Ethiopia brings most of the water and the rich silt that once flooded Egypt every year.
fact: The Aswan High Dam, finished in 1970, stopped the Nile's yearly flood, giving steady water and electricity but holding back the silt.
fact: The Yangtze is the longest river in Asia, about 6,300 km, and the Three Gorges Dam on it is the largest power station in the world by capacity.
fact: The Mississippi and its tributaries, such as the Missouri and the Ohio, drain about two-fifths of the 48 neighbouring US states and together form a system about 6,000 km long.
fact: The Volga, about 3,500 km long, is the longest river in Europe, and it flows into the Caspian Sea, not into an ocean.
fact: The Danube flows through or along the borders of 10 countries, more than any other river, and through four capitals: Vienna, Bratislava, Budapest and Belgrade.
fact: The Rhine flows from the Swiss Alps to the North Sea, and Rotterdam, near its mouth, is the largest port in Europe.
fact: The Ganges is sacred to Hindus, and together with the Brahmaputra it forms the largest river delta in the world, most of it in Bangladesh.
fact: China's Yellow River is named after the yellow-brown loess silt it carries, and that silt builds up in its bed, raising the river so it has changed its course many times, causing some of the deadliest floods in history.
fact: The Congo River is the deepest river in the world, more than 220 metres deep in places, and it carries the second most water of any river.
exercise: Which river is usually called the longest in the world? | The Nile | The Mississippi | The Danube | The Thames
exercise: Why is it hard to say whether the Nile or the Amazon is longer? | People measure from different sources and mouths and get different lengths | Both rivers are exactly the same length | Nobody has ever been to the Amazon | The Nile shrinks and grows by about 100 km every single day, so nobody can ever measure it properly
exercise: About how much of all river water flowing into the oceans comes from the Amazon? | About a fifth | About half of it | Almost all of it | About a hundredth
exercise: Where do the White Nile and the Blue Nile meet? | At Khartoum in Sudan | At Cairo in Egypt | At Lake Victoria | At the Mediterranean Sea
exercise: Which branch of the Nile brings most of its water and silt? | The Blue Nile from Ethiopia | The White Nile from Lake Victoria | The Red Nile from Arabia | The Green Nile from Chad
exercise: What did the Aswan High Dam stop? | The Nile's yearly flood | All ships on the Nile | The river from reaching Sudan | The rain in Ethiopia
exercise: Which is the longest river in Asia? | The Yangtze | The Ganges | The Volga | The Mekong
exercise: What is special about the Three Gorges Dam? | It is the largest power station in the world by capacity | It is the oldest dam in the world, built by the first emperors | It stopped the Yangtze reaching the sea | It is made only of wood
exercise: Which rivers are tributaries of the Mississippi? | The Missouri and the Ohio | The Amazon and the Nile | The Rhine and the Danube | The Hudson and the Thames
exercise: Where does the Volga end? | In the Caspian Sea | In the Arctic Ocean | In the Black Sea | In the Baltic Sea
exercise: Which is the longest river in Europe? | The Volga | The Danube | The Rhine, in Germany | The Thames
exercise: Through how many countries does the Danube flow or form borders? | 10 | 2 | 25 | 4
exercise: Which capitals lie on the Danube? | Vienna, Bratislava, Budapest and Belgrade | Paris, Berlin, Rome and Madrid | Prague, Warsaw, Kyiv, Bucharest and Minsk | London, Dublin, Oslo and Bern
exercise: Which is the largest port in Europe, near the mouth of the Rhine? | Rotterdam | Hamburg | Marseille | London
exercise: Which two rivers form the largest delta in the world? | The Ganges and the Brahmaputra | The Nile and the Congo | The Mekong and the Irrawaddy, in Myanmar | The Tigris and the Volga
exercise: Why is the Yellow River called yellow? | It carries yellow-brown loess silt | Yellow flowers grow along its banks | The Sun shines on it all day | Its water is full of gold
exercise: Which is the deepest river in the world? | The Congo | The Amazon | The Nile in Egypt | The Yangtze
test: Max says: «The Amazon is definitely longer than the Nile, my teacher said.» What is the best answer? | It is still argued: the Nile is usually named first, but some measurements make the Amazon longer | Max is right, it is proven beyond doubt | The Nile is twice as long as the Amazon | Both rivers are the same length
test: «ancient egyptians were happy about the flood?? sounds awful» Which is the best answer? | The flood left rich silt on the fields, so crops grew well | They liked swimming in it | It washed the sand away and turned the deserts into forests | It brought gold from the mountains
test: «why does the volga never get to the ocean, is it lazy lol» What is right? | It flows into the Caspian Sea, a lake with no outlet to the ocean | It dries up before it gets there | It goes underground and comes out again in the Black Sea, near Crimea | It flows uphill into the Arctic
test: In Europa Universalis 5, a river is mostly a line that slows armies. What does the game leave out? | Rivers also give water, rich farmland, fish, power and routes for boats | Nothing, rivers do nothing else | Rivers are only found in deserts | Rivers in real life cannot be crossed at all
test: What would happen if a big river kept building up silt in its bed? | The river would rise higher and flood or change course more easily | The river would get deeper and calmer | The river would turn into a lake at once | Nothing at all would change, because the silt would always wash out to sea
test: «danube's in like 3 countries max» What is right? | It flows through or along the borders of 10 countries | It is only in Austria | It is only in Germany and Austria | It flows in 50 countries
test: Why would a port at the mouth of a big river become very busy? | Goods from the whole river can be moved onto sea ships there | Rivers are saltier at the mouth | Fish only live at river mouths, so all the fishing boats gather there | Ships cannot sail on the sea
test: «bloody hell, the congo's 220 metres deep?? thats deeper than a skyscraper is tall» Is the depth right? | Yes, it is more than 220 metres deep in places, the deepest river | No, no river is more than 10 metres deep | No, it is only 2 metres deep | No, it is 22 km deep
test: Why does a river through a rainforest carry so much water? | Rainforests get heavy rain nearly all year | Rainforest trees make new water out of the soil | The sea flows backwards into it | Snow melts in the rainforest every day
test: «the ganges delta, thats in india right» What is right? | Most of the delta is in Bangladesh, with part in India | All of it is in Pakistan | It is in China | It is all in Nepal, high up near Mount Everest
test: Why did the Aswan High Dam help and harm Egypt at the same time? | It gave steady water and power but held back the rich silt | It brought floods every week but no water | It made the Nile flow backwards | It gave the fields more silt but took away all the river's water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relative lengths of the Nile and the Amazon? | The Nile is conventionally designated the longest, whereas certain measurements accord greater length to the Amazon | The Amazon is universally acknowledged to be twice the length of the Nile | The two rivers are of precisely identical length | The Nile is shorter than the Thames
exam: Identify the body of water into which the River Volga discharges. | The Caspian Sea | The Black Sea | The Baltic Sea | The Arctic Ocean
exam: State the number of states through or along whose borders the River Danube flows. | Ten | Two only | Four | Twenty
exam: Ascertain the principal consequence of the completion of the Aswan High Dam in the year 1970. | The annual inundation of the Nile was terminated, affording regular water and electricity whilst retaining the silt | The annual inundation of the Nile was rendered more severe | The Nile was diverted into the Red Sea | The Blue Nile ceased to contribute water to the Nile
